{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_dxQqQZLbj1bzhGLJPR31Dn2V","content":"300\t- A closure made inside a method keeps that method's `$this` and called class;\n301\t  a `static` closure keeps neither. `bind`, `bindTo` and `call` replace `$this`\n302\t  and the scope class, and the closure's own `self`, `static::` and visibility\n303\t  checks follow the replaced scope (`bindTo($o)` without a scope leaves the\n304\t  scope alone, `call` binds both to the object). Callable forms\n305\t  (`[$object, 'm']`, `['Class', 'm']`, `'Class::m'`, invokable objects,\n306\t  `Closure::fromCallable`) resolve through the calling class scope.\n307\t\n308\t### 3.8 Functions and the tables\n309\t\n310\tThe function table and the class table are heap dicts in `php.rt/*`, populated\n311\tas declarations execute: top-level unconditional declarations are hoisted to\n312\tthe start of the unit as PHP does, and conditional ones execute in place. A\n313\tcall to an undefined function raises `Error(\"Call to undefined function f()\")`\n314\tat run time, before any argument runs (row 53). In addition the lowering emits\n315\ta warning-severity diagnostic `:yang.php/unknown-builtin` for a name in the\n316\tpinned PHP builtin catalogue (a checked-in list) that the prelude does not\n317\tsupply, so unsupported stdlib fails honestly before the program runs (law 12).\n318\t\n319\t### 3.9 Exceptions, warnings and `@`\n320\t\n321\t- `Throwable` with the `Exception` and `Error` hierarchies (`TypeError`,\n322\t  `ValueError`, `ArgumentCountError`, `ArithmeticError`,\n323\t  `DivisionByZeroError`, `UnhandledMatchError`, `JsonException`, the SPL\n324\t  exceptions); `try`/`catch` (multi-type `|`, optional variable)/`finally`;\n325\t  `throw` as an expression (8.0).\n326\t- **Warnings, notices and deprecations are not exceptions**: they are\n327\t  `:php.diag/warning` records appended to a guest-diagnostics stream the\n328\t  composition supplies (deterministic, since they derive from program state;\n329\t  owner decision B8.4), observable by `set_error_handler`, a guest callable in\n330\t  a cell invoked at the emission point by prelude code. `ErrorException`\n331\t  conversion is then user code.\n332\t- **`@`** lowers the expression's diagnostics with `error_reporting()`\n333\t  temporarily reduced to the PHP 8 mask (`E_ERROR | E_PARSE | E_CORE_ERROR |\n334\t  E_COMPILE_ERROR | E_USER_ERROR | E_RECOVERABLE_ERROR`). A handler installed\n335\t  by `set_error_handler` is **still invoked** and observes the reduced value;\n336\t  only the default output is silenced.\n337\t- `E_ERROR` fatals (undefined class, abstract instantiation) are `Error`\n338\t  throws. The uncaught-exception text is pinned to PHP's `PHP Fatal error:\n339\t  Uncaught ...` format without the file path.\n340\t\n341\t### 3.10 Generators and iteration protocols\n342\t\n343\t`yield`, `yield k => v`, `send`, `throw`, `getReturn` and `yield from` map\n344\tonto the landed C2 core (switch, yield-raw, exit, flag cells, a per-generator\n345\thandler stack); `Generator` implements `Iterator`. `foreach` drives arrays and\n346\t`Iterator`/`IteratorAggregate` objects; `ArrayAccess` and `Countable` are\n347\tsupported. Destroying a suspended generator releases its CVs but does not run\n348\tits pending `finally` blocks (corner C8, closed by P4-c).\n349\t\n350\t### 3.11 Mixed HTML mode\n351\t\n352\t`inlineHtml` tokens lower to `php/echo` of the literal text in source order\n353\tbetween code blocks. `?>` swallows one following newline (the lexer's rule,\n354\tpreserved through the span); the first `<?php` has no output before it unless\n355\tthere is text. Output order is statement order: exit criterion 1 is a direct\n356\tfixture.\n357\t\n358\t### 3.12 eval\n359\t\n360\t`eval` is a new compilation request (`yang.antlr.md` 8.6) sharing the caller's\n361\tscope, so it forces the dynamic-scope profile on its function. It is refused\n362\twith `:yang.php/unsupported :eval` until its slice (P9).\n363\t\n364\t## 4. The reference, array and object lifecycle\n365\t\n366\tPHP's observable aliasing (references, copy-on-write separation that unwraps\n367\tunshared references, destructor timing) is defined by the Zend engine's\n368\toperations, so this model is derived from them, not from an abstraction. Each\n369\trow names the Zend handler it maps to; each row is normative and is confirmed\n370\tagainst the pinned PHP CLI before the slice that claims it lands (the blocking\n371\trule, section 17.3).\n372\t\n373\t### 4.1 What the engine does\n374\t\n375\t- A **zval** is a typed slot in a CV, a hashtable bucket, a property table or\n376\t  a temporary. Refcounted kinds carry a header: strings, arrays\n377\t  (`zend_array`), objects, resources and references (`zend_reference\n378\t  {refcount, val}`).\n379\t- **Copying a zval** (`$b = $a`, `SEND_VAR`, `RETURN`, the `FE_RESET_R`\n380\t  temporary, array-literal elements) copies the slot and `GC_ADDREF`s the\n381\t  pointee: for an array, the **hashtable**, never its elements. Literal arrays\n382\t  are immutable and uncounted; the first write duplicates.\n383\t- **Writing into an array** goes through a write-address fetch (`FETCH_DIM_W`,\n384\t  `FETCH_OBJ_W`, `FETCH_DIM_RW`): the container is separated\n385\t  (`SEPARATE_ARRAY`, `zend_array_dup`) when its refcount is above 1, a missing\n386\t  key is auto-vivified, and the element's address is returned. `$a[k] = v`,\n387\t  `$r =& $a[k]`, a by-reference `f($a[k])`, `foreach ($a as &$v)` and `$a[] =\n388\t  v` all use this path, separating each level as reached.\n389\t- **`zend_array_dup`** copies buckets; a reference with refcount 1 whose value"}
