truction vectors, \"\n854\t             \"not AST: lower and adapt it first \""}
{"role":"assistant","content":"**Diagnosis: suspect 2 holds.** `h` retains one historical continuation chain per resume. Suspended-delegator destruction is not the leak.\n\nIn `php/gen-enter`, resuming a generator builds its running state with `assoc` on the entire previous suspended state:\n\n`src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:4310`\n\nThat preserves the old `:resume` and `:ctx`. The next `php/yield-raw` captures a continuation whose environment contains this running state, so the new `:resume` reaches the previous `:resume`, which reaches the one before it. Keeping `h` therefore keeps the whole history. This explains both the linear cell growth and its identical behavior across all four VMs.\n\nBy contrast, suspended-generator destruction in `php/gen-release!` replaces the generator state, invokes cleanup for created/suspended generators, and releases `:current` (`prelude.cljc:4407-4418`). Cleanup releases the lowered CVs, including the delegate (`lower.cljc:3093-3101`). Dropping the state also drops the saved continuation and context. There is no missing delegate/CV release there.\n\n**Engineer fix:** rebuild the running state in `php/gen-enter` rather than carrying forward suspension-only fields. Preserve generator metadata and iteration state (`:name`, `:current`, `:key`, `:next-key`), add `:return` and `:caller-ctx`, but explicitly drop `:resume`, `:ctx`, and the initial `:body`. Do not try to fix this by adding destructor releases; the retained objects are reachable through the live continuation chain.\n\nThe existing N=10/N=1000 reachable-heap test is the correct regression gate. Checkpoint B **must wait for the fix**: unbounded retention under ordinary suspend/resume is a runtime defect, and closing with it open would invalidate the heap-stability requirement."}
